{
  "name": "compaction",
  "description": "Compaction outcomes (§9). Unlike merge, these depend on a whole replica's history: the horizon is derived from recorded peer frontiers, so each vector carries the log AND those frontiers. `stateUnchanged` is the invariant that matters most — compaction reclaims storage and must never alter what a reader sees.",
  "vectors": [
    {
      "name": "superseded-values-reclaimed",
      "description": "Three writes to one cell; only the last is the winner. With every peer's frontier past all three, the two losers are droppable. Materialized state must be identical before and after — that is the invariant that makes compaction safe at all.",
      "ops": [
